To better balance effectiveness and efficiency, we propose a progressive retrieval paradigm, which enables load balancing and improves retrieval accuracy by collaborating mixed-capacity retrievers, refining retrieval granularity, and reducing candidate scale. It can be formulated into three stages: 

(1) Retrieval, which usually uses relatively simple bi-encoder models to retrieve coarse-grained units; 

(2) Pre-ranking, which adopts cross-encoder models to rank the previously retrieved units; and (3) Post-ranking, which employs relatively complex list-wise models to rank these fine-grained units.

From a broader historical perspective, transfer of knowledge among humans involves 3 components, as shown in Fig. 1: (1) available (existing) knowledge; 

(2) technology for knowledge transfer, including storage, sharing and dissemination; 

(3) individual human learner using technology (2) to access knowledge (1). 

These three components are interdependent, and they have evolved throughout human history, due to technological innovations. Fig. 1(a) shows knowledge transfer in a pre-digital world, where the cost of technology for knowledge dissemination was high, so knowledge was generated by a few highly educated people. Examples include hand-written documents, the printing press, libraries etc. For such technologies, acquisition of knowledge requires understanding (or learning) by individual humans. 

Modern digital technologies, such as the World Wide Web and LLMs, have totally changed the nature of knowledge transfer. In particular, -adoption of the Web made the cost of accessing information negligibly small, leading to 'democratization' of knowledge. That is, knowledge can be easily generated and accessed by everyone. This has resulted in an increasing amount of knowledge and the need for search. 

-introduction of modern AI tools have resulted in extreme form of democratization, when transfer of knowledge (to individual humans) does not require human understanding.
